/** Generated from the overhaul textbook. Re-run tools/import-overhaul-courses.mjs. Do not edit by hand. */
export default {"id":"sounddesign-level-1","number":1,"title":"Hear First","skillBand":"Textbook","duration":"From the course textbook","summary":"- Define sound design strictly in terms of spectral composition (tone) and temporal variation (movement) without relying on preset selection. - Differentiate functional sound roles (e.g., bass, lead, pad, pluck) from specific software instr","modules":[{"id":"sounddesign-m-1-1-1","code":"1.1","title":"The Craft","blurb":"- Define sound design strictly in terms of spectral composition (tone) and temporal variation (movement) without relying on preset selection. - Differentiate functional sound roles (e.g., bass, lead, pad, pluck) from specific software instr","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"sounddesign-l-o","title":"Section overview","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"Learning Objectives","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Define sound design strictly in terms of spectral composition (tone) and temporal variation (movement) without relying on preset selection.","Differentiate functional sound roles (e.g., bass, lead, pad, pluck) from specific software instruments or synthesis engines.","Analyze presets as static parameter snapshots and apply intentional decision-making from an initialized state (<em>init</em>).","Formulate the universal signal flow model: Source → Spectral Shape → Loudness Shape → Modulation → Finish → Verify."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-1-1-1","title":"1.1.1 — Sound Design is Tone + Movement","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.1.1 — Sound Design is Tone + Movement","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Sound design in electronic music production is the deterministic manipulation of acoustic and synthetic parameters to construct, alter, and control audio signals. At its fundamental level, any designed sound is defined by two orthogonal domains:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Tone (Spectral Domain):</strong> The static frequency spectrum of the sound. This encompasses the fundamental frequency, the harmonic and inharmonic partials present, and their relative amplitude distribution across the audible spectrum (20 Hz to 20 kHz).","<strong>Movement (Temporal and Modulation Domain):</strong> How the spectral profile, amplitude, and spatial characteristics evolve over time. Movement transforms static waveforms into dynamic, expressive signals via envelopes, low-frequency oscillators (LFOs), automation, and performance controllers."],"ordered":true},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                              SOUND DESIGN                               |\n+------------------------------------+------------------------------------+\n|                TONE                |              MOVEMENT              |\n|        (Frequency Profile)         |         (Temporal Evolution)       |\n+------------------------------------+------------------------------------+\n| - Waveform harmonic selection      | - Envelope attack, decay, release  |\n| - Filter cutoff and resonance      | - LFO cyclical parameter shifts    |\n| - FM/PM sideband generation        | - Velocity and keytracking dynamics|\n| - Waveshaping and saturation       | - Macro-driven performance control |\n+------------------------------------+------------------------------------+</pre>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"The Subtractive Sculptor Model","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The primary paradigm for foundational sound design is <strong>subtractive synthesis</strong>:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>The Raw Block (Source):</strong> An oscillator generates a waveform rich in harmonic overtones (e.g., sawtooth or pulse).","<strong>The Chisel (Spectral Filter):</strong> A filter attenuates specific frequency bands, carving away unwanted spectral energy.","<strong>The Contour (Loudness Shape):</strong> An amplifier envelope (VCA/AEG) dictates the amplitude contour from note onset to release.","<strong>The Animation (Modulation):</strong> LFOs and auxiliary envelopes modulate filter cutoff, pitch, or pulse width to introduce continuous movement."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">[ OSCILLATOR(S) ] ---&gt; [ FILTER (VCF) ] ---&gt; [ AMPLIFIER (VCA) ] ---&gt; [ AUDIO OUT ]\n      ^                       ^                       ^\n      |                       |                       |\n [ Pitch LFO ]         [ Filter Env / LFO ]     [ Amp Envelope ]</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Try It:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["Open Ableton Live 12 and insert <code>Drift</code> onto an empty MIDI track.","Ensure Oscillator 1 is set to a <strong>Saw</strong> wave, the Filter is in <strong>Low-pass (LP12)</strong> mode, and the Cutoff is set fully open (20 kHz).","Hold down a sustained MIDI note (C_2). Observe the bright, static tone.","Manually reduce the Filter Cutoff from 20 kHz down to 100 Hz over a 5-second interval. Note how the upper harmonics are systematically removed, shifting the timbre from harsh and buzzy to muted and round. This is pure spectral sculpting without temporal automation."],"ordered":true},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Ultimate Sound Design: Mastering Sound Design</em>, Section 1 (Foundations &amp; Approach)","<em>Sound-Design-Beginner-Knowledgebase.md</em>, §1 &amp; §2"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-1-1-2","title":"1.1.2 — Roles vs. Instruments (A Bass is a Job, Not a Plugin)","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.1.2 — Roles vs. Instruments (A Bass is a Job, Not a Plugin)","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"A frequent misconception among beginner producers is treating sound categories as proprietary plugin features. In a production environment, a sound is defined exclusively by its <strong>functional acoustic role</strong> within the frequency arrangement, not by the instrument plugin generating it."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+---------------+-------------------------------------+-------------------------------------+\n| Functional    | Primary Frequency Band              | Envelope &amp; Spectral Characteristics |\n| Role          | &amp; Spatial Profile                   |                                     |\n+---------------+-------------------------------------+-------------------------------------+\n| **Sub Bass**  | $30\\text{ Hz} - 80\\text{ Hz}$       | Fast attack, high sustain;          |\n|               | Mono, centered                      | pure sine/triangle fundamental      |\n+---------------+-------------------------------------+-------------------------------------+\n| **Mid Bass**  | $80\\text{ Hz} - 1\\text{ kHz}$       | Dynamic filter decay;               |\n|               | Controlled stereo spread            | rich harmonic saturation/movement   |\n+---------------+-------------------------------------+-------------------------------------+\n| **Pluck**     | $200\\text{ Hz} - 8\\text{ kHz}$      | Instant attack, short decay ($&lt;400\\text{ ms}$),|\n|               | Dynamic stereo placement            | zero sustain; sharp filter decay    |\n+---------------+-------------------------------------+-------------------------------------+\n| **Pad**       | $150\\text{ Hz} - 12\\text{ kHz}$     | Slow attack ($&gt;300\\text{ ms}$),     |\n|               | Wide stereo image                   | high sustain, long release          |\n+---------------+-------------------------------------+-------------------------------------+\n| **Lead**      | $500\\text{ Hz} - 10\\text{ kHz}$     | Instant/short attack, moderate      |\n|               | Centered / focused width            | sustain; monophonic with glide      |\n+---------------+-------------------------------------+-------------------------------------+</pre>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Engine Independence","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The same functional bass or pluck can be constructed in:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Ableton Operator:</strong> Using 4-operator Phase Modulation (FM).","<strong>Vital:</strong> Using wavetable scanning and spectral warping.","<strong>Surge XT:</strong> Using multi-scene modern subtractive or audio-rate FM oscillators.","<strong>Ableton Analog/Drift:</strong> Using classic dual-oscillator virtual analog routing."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"Synthesizer choice dictates the <em>flavor</em> and <em>workflow efficiency</em> of the generation method, but the physical parameters that make a sound perform its role remain constant across all engines."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Try It:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["Maintain a single monophonic MIDI sequence of 1/8th notes on pitch D_1.","In <code>Drift</code>, set the Amp Envelope to: Attack = 0 ms, Decay = 150 ms, Sustain = -inf dB, Release = 50 ms. Set the Filter Cutoff low (300 Hz) with high envelope modulation (+5.00). Notice how the sound functions as a punchy, percussive bass pluck.","Now adjust only the Amp Envelope parameters: Attack = 400 ms, Decay = 1.0 s, Sustain = 0.0 dB, Release = 800 ms. Open the Filter Cutoff manually.","Without changing the oscillator waveform or the MIDI pattern, the sound ceases to function as a rhythmic bass engine and transforms into a continuous harmonic drone."],"ordered":true},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Ultimate Sound Design: Mastering Sound Design</em>, Section 1.2 (Sound Roles)","<em>Sound-Design-Beginner-Knowledgebase.md</em>, §8"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-1-1-3","title":"1.1.3 — Presets are Snapshots; Design is Decisions","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.1.3 — Presets are Snapshots; Design is Decisions","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"A preset is nothing more than a static serialization of parameter values saved to a configuration file. Relying entirely on browsing presets introduces three critical engineering bottlenecks:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Context Blindness:</strong> A preset is crafted in isolation with arbitrary gain staging, fixed frequency resonances, and preset-specific reverb/delay decays that often clash with the track's tempo, key, and arrangement density.","<strong>Inflexible Modulations:</strong> When a preset contains excessive modulation matrix assignments, altering a single aspect (e.g., shortening a decay time) often breaks unmapped interdependencies.","<strong>Inefficient Workflow:</strong> Spending 30 minutes auditioning presets is mathematically slower than spending 60 seconds building the precise envelope and harmonic target from an initialized state."],"ordered":true},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">                       PRESET BROWSING vs. SOUND DESIGN\n                       \n[ Preset Workflow ]   Browse 500 Presets -&gt; Pick Closest Match -&gt; Fight Mix Clashes -&gt; Compromise\n[ Design Workflow ]   Identify Role -&gt; Select Engine -&gt; Set Harmonic Source -&gt; Shape Envelope -&gt; Finish</pre>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"The Init-Patch Protocol","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Professional sound design begins with an <strong>initialized patch (<code>init</code>)</strong>:"},{"type":"list","items":["All oscillators set to basic waveforms (typically a single Saw or Sine wave) with unmodulated pitch.","All filters fully open or disabled with zero resonance.","Amplitude envelope set to immediate attack, full sustain, and short release.","All internal effects (reverbs, delays, chorus, distortion) disabled.","Modulation matrices and LFO assignments fully cleared."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"Every knob turn from an <code>init</code> state represents a deliberate engineering decision addressing a specific sonic requirement."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Try It:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["In Ableton Live, load <code>Analog</code>. Right-click the title bar and select <strong>Preset -&gt; Load Default Preset</strong> (or manually configure an init state: Osc 1 Saw, Osc 2 Off, Filter 1 Off, Amp 1 Sustain 1.0, Effects Off).","Play a note. Verify that there are zero modulations, zero stereo chorusing, and zero frequency roll-offs.","Make three isolated decisions:","Decision 1: Drop Osc 1 tuning by -1 Octave (establishes low-end register).","Decision 2: Engage Filter 1 as Low-pass 24 dB/oct, set Cutoff to 400 Hz (eliminates high frequency presence).","Decision 3: Set Amp 1 Sustain to 0.0 and Decay to 250 ms (enforces percussive decay).","You have constructed a functional sub/mid bass foundation in under ten seconds through three conscious decisions."],"ordered":true},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Ultimate Sound Design: Mastering Sound Design</em>, Section 1.3 (The Init Discipline)","<em>Ableton Live 12 Reference Manual</em>, Chapter 23 (Live Instrument Reference)"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-1-1-4","title":"1.1.4 — Principles First, Then Crank Knobs on Purpose","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.1.4 — Principles First, Then Crank Knobs on Purpose","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Sound design mastery is not achieved through timid incremental tweaks, nor through arbitrary chaos. It follows a systematic learning and implementation protocol:"},{"type":"prose","html":"Acoustic Principle Isolated Testing Extreme Boundary Exploration Precise Application"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+----------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                       SIX-STEP PRODUCTION SIGNAL FLOW                      |\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| 1. SOURCE: Select basic generator (Oscillator, Noise, Sample, Physical)    |\n| 2. SPECTRAL SHAPE: Apply filtering, waveshaping, or FM/Warp modifiers      |\n| 3. LOUDNESS SHAPE: Set Amplitude ADSR to define temporal role              |\n| 4. MODULATION: Route LFOs, Envelopes, or Velocity to targeted parameters   |\n| 5. FINISH: Apply dynamics, saturation, spatial positioning, and EQ         |\n| 6. VERIFY: Evaluate in solo, in mix context, and via frequency spectrum    |\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------+</pre>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Boundary Testing Discipline","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"When learning any new control or crafting a patch:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Locate the control</strong> in the synth architecture.","<strong>Drive the control to its minimum (0%)</strong> and listen to what disappears.","<strong>Drive the control to its maximum (100%)</strong> and listen to what distorts, rings, or saturates.","<strong>Dial the control into the precise sweet spot</strong> required for the mix."],"ordered":true},{"type":"prose","html":"Never adjust two independent parameters simultaneously during the initial sound staging. If you alter the filter cutoff while simultaneously adjusting the amplifier decay, you cannot reliably discern which parameter resolved an acoustic deficiency."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Practice Exercise:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["Set a timer for 10 minutes.","Load <code>Drift</code> on an initialized patch.","Restrict yourself to adjusting only <strong>two</strong> controls: <code>Filter Cutoff</code> and <code>Filter Resonance</code>.","Play a continuous rhythmic phrase and practice sweeping between subtle warmth (Cutoff 800 Hz, Res 0.1), vocal-like resonance peaks (Cutoff 1.2 kHz, Res 0.7), and aggressive self-oscillation whistling (Cutoff 2.5 kHz, Res 0.95)."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Ultimate Sound Design: Mastering Sound Design</em>, Section 1.4 (The Decision Matrix)","<em>Sound-Design-Beginner-Knowledgebase.md</em>, §1.1 &amp; §14"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"sounddesign-m-1-2-2","code":"1.2","title":"Your Studio Map","blurb":"- Map the hardware, DAW host, and plugin infrastructure of this dedicated production studio. - Verify the structural loading and routing of the three primary synthesis engines: Ableton Operator, Vital, and Surge XT.","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"sounddesign-l-o-2","title":"Section overview","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"Learning Objectives","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Map the hardware, DAW host, and plugin infrastructure of this dedicated production studio.","Verify the structural loading and routing of the three primary synthesis engines: <strong>Ableton Operator</strong>, <strong>Vital</strong>, and <strong>Surge XT</strong>.","Differentiate early training instruments (<code>Analog</code>, <code>Drift</code>) from deep specialized synthesis engines.","Configure the <strong>Novation Launchkey MK4 61</strong> as an interactive sound-design surface via Plugin Mode.","Establish stable Windows 11 ASIO audio parameters and build a persistent User Library patch archive."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-1-2-1","title":"1.2.1 — Live as Host: MIDI Track → Instrument → Audio Effects","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.2.1 — Live as Host: MIDI Track → Instrument → Audio Effects","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"In Ableton Live 12, sound generation and signal processing follow a strict, linear left-to-right device architecture. Understanding this chain is essential for preventing signal flow bottlenecks."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                                    ABLETON MIDI TRACK                                   |\n+---------------------+-------------------------------+-----------------------------------+\n|    MIDI EFFECTS     |          INSTRUMENT           |           AUDIO EFFECTS           |\n| (Alters Note Data)  |      (Generates Audio)        |       (Processes Audio Signal)    |\n+---------------------+-------------------------------+-----------------------------------+\n| - Arpeggiator       | - Operator (FM / Additive)    | - EQ Eight (Frequency Sculpting)  |\n| - Scale             | - Drift / Analog (Subtractive)| - Auto Filter (Dynamic Movement)  |\n| - Chord             | - Vital (Wavetable / Warp)    | - Saturator / Drive (Harmonics)   |\n| - Velocity          | - Surge XT (Multi-Engine)     | - Reverb / Delay (Space / Time)   |\n|                     | - Simpler (Sampling)          | - Utility (Gain / Mono Low End)   |\n+---------------------+-------------------------------+-----------------------------------+\n        [ MIDI ]  ========&gt;       [ AUDIO SIGNAL ]  ========&gt;       [ TRACK OUTPUT ]</pre>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"The Rules of the Signal Chain","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>MIDI Effects Precede the Instrument:</strong> MIDI devices (e.g., Arpeggiator, Scale, Chord) operate strictly on note event data (pitch, velocity, note-on/off messages). They output MIDI, not audio.","<strong>The Instrument Converts Domain:</strong> The instrument (stock or third-party VST3) sits in the center of the track. It receives incoming MIDI events and renders digital audio. You cannot place audio effects before an instrument on a MIDI track.","<strong>Audio Effects Succeed the Instrument:</strong> All spectral, dynamic, and temporal processing (filters, compressors, saturators, delays) must occur <em>after</em> the instrument stage.","<strong>Audio Tracks Cannot Host Instruments:</strong> Audio tracks accept only audio clips and audio effect devices; they cannot interpret MIDI note data directly."],"ordered":true},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Ableton Live 12 Reference Manual</em>, Chapter 23 (Working with Instruments and Effects)","<em>Sound-Design-Beginner-Knowledgebase.md</em>, §8 &amp; §10"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-1-2-2","title":"1.2.2 — The Three Engines: Operator, Vital, Surge XT (Load Test)","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.2.2 — The Three Engines: Operator, Vital, Surge XT (Load Test)","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"This curriculum anchors all advanced sound design across three specialized, production-standard engines. Each represents a distinct synthesis methodology:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+------------------+-----------------------+---------------------------------------------------+\n| Engine           | Core Architecture     | Primary Sound Design Application                  |\n+------------------+-----------------------+---------------------------------------------------+\n| **Operator**     | 4-Operator FM / PM,   | Deterministic FM basslines, bright metallic       |\n| (Ableton Add-on) | Additive Editor       | house plucks, additive bells, synthetic drums     |\n+------------------+-----------------------+---------------------------------------------------+\n| **Vital**        | Wavetable Morphing,   | Modern aggressive bass growls, morphing pads,     |\n| (VST3)           | Spectral Warps        | multi-voice supersaws, visual drag-and-drop matrix|\n+------------------+-----------------------+---------------------------------------------------+\n| **Surge XT**     | Hybrid Multi-Engine,  | Classic/Modern analog models, multi-scene layers, |\n| (Open-Source/VST3)| Dual Filter Routing   | Reese basses, audio-rate modulation, deep routing |\n+------------------+-----------------------+---------------------------------------------------+</pre>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Verification and Load Protocol","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Every engine must be confirmed operational in 64-bit VST3 or native format within Ableton Live 12:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">[ Live Browser ] -&gt; Plug-Ins -&gt; VST3 -&gt; Surge Synth Team -&gt; Surge XT\n[ Live Browser ] -&gt; Plug-Ins -&gt; VST3 -&gt; Matt Tytel -&gt; Vital\n[ Live Browser ] -&gt; Instruments -&gt; Operator</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Try It (Load &amp; Audio Test):</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["Create three separate MIDI tracks in Ableton Live.","Load <code>Operator</code> on Track 1. Play a MIDI note C_3. Verify pure sine wave playback.","Load <code>Vital</code> on Track 2. Open the GUI window. Play C_3. Verify the default initialized wavetable plays cleanly.","Load <code>Surge XT</code> on Track 3. Open the GUI. Play C_3. Verify Scene A oscillator outputs sound.","Mute all tracks and confirm zero background CPU spikes."],"ordered":true},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Ableton Live 12 Reference Manual</em>, Chapter 23 (Live Instrument Reference: Operator)","<em>Vital User Manual</em>, Section 1 (Installation &amp; Overview)","<em>Surge XT User Manual</em>, Section 1 (Architecture &amp; Setup)"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-1-2-3","title":"1.2.3 — Analog and Drift as Early Trainers","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.2.3 — Analog and Drift as Early Trainers","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Before addressing complex multi-operator FM or modular wavetable matrices, foundational subtractive literacy must be mastered on focused, immediate interfaces. Live 12 Standard includes two ideal subtractive trainers:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-----------------------------------+-----------------------------------+\n|              DRIFT                |              ANALOG               |\n|      (Immediate &amp; Minimalist)     |    (Classic Dual Virtual Analog)  |\n+-----------------------------------+-----------------------------------+\n| - 2 Oscillators + Noise           | - 2 Independent Oscillators + Sub |\n| - State-Variable Filter (Morphing)| - 2 Independent Multi-mode Filters|\n| - 2 ADSR Envelopes + 1 LFO        | - 2 Dedicated Loopable Envelopes  |\n| - Integrated Drift Pitch Instability| - Flexible Serial/Parallel Routing|\n| - Best for: Quick subtractive     | - Best for: Deep dual-path        |\n|   sketches and basic patch math   |   classic subtractive architecture|\n+-----------------------------------+-----------------------------------+</pre>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Educational Role","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong><code>Drift</code></strong> eliminates visual clutter and routing complexity, allowing you to hear exact cutoff, resonance, and envelope interactions instantly.","<strong><code>Analog</code></strong> introduces dual-filter routing (Series vs. Parallel) and dedicated per-oscillator amplifier envelopes, establishing the mental framework needed for complex synths like Surge XT."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Ableton Live 12 Reference Manual</em>, Chapter 23 (Drift &amp; Analog)","<em>Sound-Design-Beginner-Knowledgebase.md</em>, §6.2"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-1-2-4","title":"1.2.4 — Launchkey MK4 61: Keys + Plugin Mode Surface","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.2.4 — Launchkey MK4 61: Keys + Plugin Mode Surface","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The Novation Launchkey MK4 61 serves as the primary physical tactile interface for sound design in this studio. It is crucial to understand the physical and functional distinction between a <strong>MIDI controller</strong> and an <strong>audio interface</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                       NOVATION LAUNCHKEY MK4 61 ROLES                         |\n+------------------------------------+------------------------------------------+\n|          WHAT IT IS                |             WHAT IT IS NOT               |\n+------------------------------------+------------------------------------------+\n| - MIDI event generator (Notes/Vel) | - NOT an audio interface                 |\n| - 8 continuous rotary encoders     | - Does NOT process or output sound       |\n| - 16 velocity-sensitive RGB pads   | - Does NOT dictate audio buffer latency  |\n| - Hardware DAW &amp; Plugin controller | - Does NOT require custom remote scripts |\n+------------------------------------+------------------------------------------+</pre>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Plugin Mode Architecture","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"When working with native Live instruments or third-party VST3 plugins:"},{"type":"list","items":["Hold <code>Shift</code> on the Launchkey and press the pad assigned to <strong>Plugin Mode</strong>.","Select any instrument or effect device in Ableton Live. The 8 encoders immediately bind to the first 8 active parameters of that device.","Use the <strong>Bank Left/Right</strong> buttons to page through additional banks of 8 parameters.","Use <code>Shift + Bank</code> to cycle through different devices along the current track's signal chain."],"ordered":true},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">[ Launchkey Encoders 1-8 ] ===(USB MIDI)===&gt; [ Ableton Focused Device ] ===&gt; [ Parameter Automation ]\n   (Encoders 1-4: Cutoff, Res, Env Amt, Decay | Encoders 5-8: LFO Rate, Depth, Drive, Level)</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Novation Launchkey MK4 61 User Guide</em>, Chapter 4 (DAW Control &amp; Plugin Mode)","<em>Sound-Design-Beginner-Knowledgebase.md</em>, §9 &amp; §12"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-1-2-5","title":"1.2.5 — Windows Audio: ASIO, Sample Rate, and Buffer Size","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.2.5 — Windows Audio: ASIO, Sample Rate, and Buffer Size","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Accurate, responsive sound design requires minimal latency between physical keypress and acoustic monitoring. On Windows 11, professional low-latency operation relies on the <strong>ASIO (Audio Stream Input/Output)</strong> driver architecture."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                       AUDIO BUFFER / LATENCY TRADE-OFF                        |\n+------------------------------------+------------------------------------------+\n| BUFFER SIZE (Samples)              | SYSTEM BEHAVIOR &amp; APPLICATION            |\n+------------------------------------+------------------------------------------+\n| **64 – 128 samples**               | Ultra-low latency ($&lt;5\\text{ ms}$).      |\n|                                    | High CPU load; risk of audio dropouts.   |\n+------------------------------------+------------------------------------------+\n| **256 samples** [RECOMMENDED]      | Optimal balance ($\\approx 6-8\\text{ ms}$).|\n|                                    | Snappy keyboard response, stable CPU.    |\n+------------------------------------+------------------------------------------+\n| **512 – 1024 samples**             | Higher latency ($&gt;15\\text{ ms}$).        |\n|                                    | Safe for heavy mixing/dense processing.  |\n+------------------------------------+------------------------------------------+</pre>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Audio Configuration Rules","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Sample Rate Match:</strong> Standardize project sample rates to <strong>48.0 kHz</strong> (or 44.1 kHz) across Windows System Sounds, Audio Interface Hardware Setup, Voicemeeter (if active), and Ableton Live (<code>Preferences -&gt; Audio</code>). Mismatches cause non-neutral real-time sample rate conversion artifacts.","<strong>Buffer Setting:</strong> Lock the ASIO buffer to <strong>256 samples</strong> during sound design tracking. If audio dropouts (crackles/pops) occur, raise to 512 samples.","<strong>Hardware Setup Access:</strong> If Buffer Size is greyed out inside Ableton Live Preferences, click <strong>Hardware Setup</strong> to configure the buffer directly within your audio interface's dedicated driver control panel."],"ordered":true},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Ableton Help: Optimizing Windows for Audio</em>","<em>Sound-Design-Beginner-Knowledgebase.md</em>, §10 &amp; §13"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-1-2-6","title":"1.2.6 — User Library / Saving Your Own Patches","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.2.6 — User Library / Saving Your Own Patches","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Sound design efficiency depends on building an organized, persistent repository of custom initialized templates, device presets, and Instrument Racks."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">User Library/\n├── Presets/\n│   └── Instruments/\n│       ├── Drift/\n│       │   ├── SD_Init_Drift.adv\n│       │   └── SD_Sub_SineClean.adv\n│       ├── Operator/\n│       │   ├── SD_Init_Operator.adv\n│       │   └── SD_FM_Pluck_Classic.adv\n│       └── Instrument Rack/\n│           ├── SD_Rack_LayeredBass.adv\n│           └── SD_Rack_SuperSawStack.adv</pre>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Standard Patch Naming Protocol","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Adopt a systematic naming taxonomy for every saved user patch: SD\\_[Category]\\_[Role/Descriptor]\\_[Engine/Version]"},{"type":"prose","html":"Examples:"},{"type":"list","items":["<code>SD_BASS_CleanMonoSub_Drift.adv</code>","<code>SD_PLUCK_HouseFM_Operator.adv</code>","<code>SD_LEAD_DualSawGlide_SurgeXT.adv</code>"],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Try It:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["In Ableton Live, create an initialized patch in <code>Drift</code> (Osc 1 Saw, Filter Open, Amp Sustain 1.0).","Click the <strong>Save Preset</strong> icon (disk icon in the upper right corner of Drift's device title bar).","Live highlights the User Library in the browser. Type <code>SD_Init_Drift</code> and hit Enter.","You now have an instantaneous, uncorrupted starting point for any future subtractive patch."],"ordered":true},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Ableton Live 12 Reference Manual</em>, Chapter 5 (Managing Files and Sets)","<em>Sound-Design-Beginner-Knowledgebase.md</em>, §14"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"sounddesign-m-1-3-3","code":"1.3","title":"Physics of Sound for Designers (Minimum Math)","blurb":"- Define the physical relationship between cyclical air pressure oscillation (waveform), frequency (Hertz), and perceived musical pitch. - Articulate timbre as the acoustic property that differentiates two signals sharing identical fundamen","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"sounddesign-l-o-3","title":"Section overview","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"Learning Objectives","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Define the physical relationship between cyclical air pressure oscillation (waveform), frequency (Hertz), and perceived musical pitch.","Articulate <strong>timbre</strong> as the acoustic property that differentiates two signals sharing identical fundamental pitch and amplitude.","Differentiate and correctly apply the acoustic terminology: <strong>fundamental</strong>, <strong>partials</strong>, <strong>harmonics</strong>, <strong>overtones</strong>, and <strong>subharmonics</strong>.","Interpret the integer-multiple structure of the <strong>harmonic series</strong> (f, 2f, 3f, 4f,) and explain its mathematical link to musical intervals.","Utilize real-time visual analysis tools (<strong>Ableton Spectrum</strong>, <strong>Voxengo SPAN</strong>) to verify ear training."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-1-3-1","title":"1.3.1 — What a Waveform is (Cycle, Rate, Pitch)","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.3.1 — What a Waveform is (Cycle, Rate, Pitch)","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Sound is a mechanical pressure wave propagating through an elastic medium (air). In synthesizer design, physical air compression and rarefaction are represented by an electrical or digital voltage signal called a <strong>waveform</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">    +1.0 |      /\\              /\\              /\\\n         |     /  \\            /  \\            /  \\\n     0.0 |----+----+----------+----+----------+----+----&gt; Time (s)\n         |   /      \\        /      \\        /      \\\n    -1.0 |  /        \\______/        \\______/        \\\n         |&lt;--- 1 Cycle ------&gt;|</pre>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Core Acoustic Parameters","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Cycle:</strong> One complete repetition of a periodic wave pattern from zero, through positive maximum, through negative minimum, returning to zero.","<strong>Period (T):</strong> The duration in seconds required to complete one single cycle (T = 1f).","<strong>Frequency (f):</strong> The number of completed cycles per second, measured in <strong>Hertz (Hz)</strong>.","<strong>Pitch:</strong> The human psychoacoustic perception of frequency. Doubling the frequency results in an increase of exactly one musical <strong>octave</strong> (12 semitones)."],"ordered":true},{"type":"prose","html":"Pitch Example: A_1 = 55 Hz × 2 A_2 = 110 Hz × 2 A_3 = 220 Hz × 2 A_4 = 440 Hz"},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Ultimate Sound Design: Mastering Sound Design</em>, Section 1.5 (Acoustic Foundations)","<em>Sound-Design-Beginner-Knowledgebase.md</em>, §2.1"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-1-3-2","title":"1.3.2 — Timbre: The Color of Sound","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.3.2 — Timbre: The Color of Sound","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"If an acoustic grand piano, a distorted electric guitar, and an initialized synthesizer all play middle C (C_3 ≈ 130.81 Hz) at precisely -14 dBFS, a human listener instantly identifies each instrument. The quality that enables this distinction is <strong>timbre</strong> (tone color)."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                             WHAT DEFINES TIMBRE?                              |\n+------------------------------------+------------------------------------------+\n| SPECTRAL CONTENT                   | Which specific frequencies are present   |\n| (Harmonics &amp; Partials)             | above the fundamental, and at what       |\n|                                    | individual amplitudes?                   |\n+------------------------------------+------------------------------------------+\n| SPECTRAL ENVELOPE                  | How does the energy balance between low, |\n| (Formants &amp; Filtering)             | mid, and high frequencies sit statically?|\n+------------------------------------+------------------------------------------+\n| TIME-VARIANT EVOLUTION             | How quickly do individual harmonics      |\n| (Dynamic Modulation)               | decay relative to the fundamental?       |\n+------------------------------------+------------------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"Timbre is the primary domain of the sound designer. While a composer decides <em>which note</em> to trigger, the sound designer dictates the <em>spectral architecture</em> of the sound."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Ultimate Sound Design: Mastering Sound Design</em>, Section 1.6 (Timbre &amp; Partials)"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-1-3-3","title":"1.3.3 — Fundamental vs. The \"Extra Stuff\"","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.3.3 — Fundamental vs. The \"Extra Stuff\"","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Every periodic musical sound consists of two distinct components:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>The Fundamental Frequency (f_0):</strong> The lowest frequency component present in a periodic waveform. It determines the perceived musical note/pitch of the sound. In a bass playing E_1 (41.2 Hz), the fundamental is the physical vibration oscillating at 41.2 times per second.","<strong>The Upper Spectrum (\"The Extra Stuff\"):</strong> All acoustic energy residing at frequencies higher than the fundamental. This upper energy provides brightness, bite, body, resonance, and harmonic richness."],"ordered":true},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">Amplitude\n  ^\n  |   | [Fundamental: Determines Note / Pitch]\n  |   |\n  |   |       |   [Upper Spectrum: Determines Brightness, Timbre, and Grit]\n  |   |   |   |   |   |   |   |\n  +---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---&gt; Frequency (Hz)\n     f_0 2f_0 3f_0 4f_0 5f_0 ...</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"A pure sine wave consists <em>exclusively</em> of the fundamental with zero upper spectrum. A sawtooth wave contains the fundamental plus a dense continuum of upper harmonics."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Ultimate Sound Design: Mastering Sound Design</em>, Section 1.7 (Fundamentals)","<em>Sound-Design-Beginner-Knowledgebase.md</em>, §2.1"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-1-3-4","title":"1.3.4 — Partials, Harmonics, Overtones: Strict vs. Practical Language","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.3.4 — Partials, Harmonics, Overtones: Strict vs. Practical Language","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"In synthesizer manuals and production discourse, the terms <em>partial</em>, <em>harmonic</em>, and <em>overtone</em> are often used interchangeably, leading to confusion. Their precise scientific definitions are:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Term             | Formal Definition                                                  |\n+------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| **Partial**      | Any individual sine wave component present in a complex sound,     |\n|                  | whether it is mathematically harmonic or inharmonic.               |\n+------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| **Harmonic**     | A partial whose frequency is an exact positive integer multiple    |\n|                  | ($1, 2, 3, 4, \\dots$) of the fundamental frequency ($n \\cdot f_0$).|\n+------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| **Overtone**     | Any frequency component *above* the fundamental. The 1st overtone  |\n|                  | equals the 2nd harmonic ($2f_0$).                                  |\n+------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| **Inharmonic**   | A partial whose frequency is NOT an integer multiple (e.g.,        |\n| **Partial**      | $1.414 \\cdot f_0$). Produces metallic, clangorous, or noisy tones.|\n+------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"Harmonic Index Mapping: Fundamental = 1^st Harmonic = f_0 First Overtone = 2^nd Harmonic = 2f_0 Second Overtone = 3^rd Harmonic = 3f_0"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+--------------------+---------------------+---------------------+\n| Frequency Multiple | Harmonic Term       | Overtone Term       |\n+--------------------+---------------------+---------------------+\n| $1 \\times f_0$     | 1st Harmonic (Fund) | —                   |\n| $2 \\times f_0$     | 2nd Harmonic        | 1st Overtone        |\n| $3 \\times f_0$     | 3rd Harmonic        | 2nd Overtone        |\n| $4 \\times f_0$     | 4th Harmonic        | 3rd Overtone        |\n+--------------------+---------------------+---------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Ultimate Sound Design: Mastering Sound Design</em>, Section 1.8 (Harmonic Nomenclature)"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-1-3-5","title":"1.3.5 — The Harmonic / Overtone Series","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.3.5 — The Harmonic / Overtone Series","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"When a physical string vibrates or an analog oscillator generates a sawtooth wave, the resulting frequencies conform to the <strong>Harmonic Series</strong>:"},{"type":"prose","html":"f_n = n · f_0 for n \\1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, \\"},{"type":"prose","html":"If the fundamental is A_1 = 110 Hz, the series generates:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+----------+--------------------+---------------------+------------------------+\n| Harmonic | Calculation        | Frequency           | Musical Interval from  |\n| Number   |                    |                     | Fundamental            |\n+----------+--------------------+---------------------+------------------------+\n| $n = 1$  | $1 \\times 110\\text{ Hz}$ | $110\\text{ Hz}$     | Fundamental (Unison)   |\n| $n = 2$  | $2 \\times 110\\text{ Hz}$ | $220\\text{ Hz}$     | Perfect Octave (+12 st)|\n| $n = 3$  | $3 \\times 110\\text{ Hz}$ | $330\\text{ Hz}$     | Perfect 5th (+19 st)   |\n| $n = 4$  | $4 \\times 110\\text{ Hz}$ | $440\\text{ Hz}$     | 2nd Octave (+24 st)    |\n| $n = 5$  | $5 \\times 110\\text{ Hz}$ | $550\\text{ Hz}$     | Major 3rd (+28 st)     |\n| $n = 6$  | $6 \\times 110\\text{ Hz}$ | $660\\text{ Hz}$     | Perfect 5th (+31 st)   |\n| $n = 7$  | $7 \\times 110\\text{ Hz}$ | $770\\text{ Hz}$     | Harmonic 7th (flat)    |\n| $n = 8$  | $8 \\times 110\\text{ Hz}$ | $880\\text{ Hz}$     | 3rd Octave (+36 st)    |\n+----------+--------------------+---------------------+------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"Notice the natural physical hierarchy: the first intervals formed by the harmonic series are the <strong>Octave</strong> and the <strong>Perfect 5th</strong>. This physical fact explains why coarse frequency ratios of 1.0, 2.0, and 3.0 in FM synthesis (Operator) sound clean, consonant, and musical."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Ultimate Sound Design: Mastering Sound Design</em>, Section 1.9 (The Overtone Ladder)"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-1-3-6","title":"1.3.6 — Subharmonics","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.3.6 — Subharmonics","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"While harmonics exist at integer multiples <em>above</em> the fundamental (n · f_0), <strong>subharmonics</strong> exist at exact integer divisions <em>below</em> the fundamental:"},{"type":"prose","html":"f_sub = f_0n for n \\2, 3, 4, \\"},{"type":"prose","html":"Example for f_0 = 440 Hz (A_4): 4402 = 220 Hz (A_3), 4403 ≈ 146.67 Hz (D_3), 4404 = 110 Hz (A_2)"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Application in Modern Synths","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Standard analog synthesizers generate a subharmonic one or two octaves down (f_02 or f_04) using a digital flip-flop circuit, commonly called a <strong>Sub-Oscillator</strong>.","Sub-oscillators are standard in <code>Analog</code>, <code>Drift</code>, and <code>Surge XT</code> to inject clean foundational weight beneath complex, high-frequency lead or pluck patches."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Ultimate Sound Design: Mastering Sound Design</em>, Section 1.10 (Subharmonics &amp; Sub-Oscillators)"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-1-3-7","title":"1.3.7 — Spectrum and Spectrogram as Design Mirrors","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.3.7 — Spectrum and Spectrogram as Design Mirrors","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Ear training must be anchored by objective visual feedback. Ableton Live 12 provides the native <strong>Spectrum</strong> device, and the studio includes <strong>Voxengo SPAN</strong> (VST3) for detailed frequency analysis."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                    ABLETON SPECTRUM DISPLAY (2D FFT)                   |\n| 0 dB |                                                                  |\n|      |       /\\                                                         |\n|      |      /  \\        /\\                                              |\n|      |     /    \\      /  \\        /\\        /\\                         |\n|-36 dB|    /      \\____/    \\______/  \\______/  \\                        |\n|      +----+---------+----------+----------+---------------------------+ |\n|          50 Hz     200 Hz     1 kHz      5 kHz           20 kHz         |\n|         [SUB]      [LOW]      [MID]      [HIGH]          [AIR]          |\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------+</pre>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Spectrum vs. Spectrogram","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Spectrum (2D FFT Plot):</strong> Graphs Frequency on the horizontal X-axis (20 Hz - 20 kHz) and Amplitude on the vertical Y-axis (-inf to 0 dBFS). It shows a real-time cross-section of harmonic distribution.","<strong>Spectrogram (3D Waterfall Plot):</strong> Graphs Frequency on the Y-axis, Time on the X-axis, and Amplitude as color brightness/heat. It illustrates how harmonics decay and evolve over time."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Try It (Visual Harmonic Verification):</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["Place <code>Drift</code> on a MIDI track. Follow it immediately with <code>Spectrum</code>.","Configure Drift: Osc 1 to <strong>Sine</strong>, Filter Cutoff fully open.","Play A_2 (110 Hz). Observe the Spectrum display: you will see a single sharp peak at 110 Hz and complete silence across the rest of the spectrum.","Switch Osc 1 to <strong>Saw</strong>. Observe the Spectrum instantly populate with an entire series of descending peaks at 220 Hz, 330 Hz, 440 Hz, 550 Hz,","Engage the Low-pass filter and lower the cutoff. Watch the high-frequency peaks disappear from right to left while the fundamental peak at 110 Hz remains untouched."],"ordered":true},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Ableton Live 12 Reference Manual</em>, Chapter 28 (Audio Effect Reference: Spectrum)","<em>Voxengo SPAN User Guide</em>, Section 1 (FFT Spectrum Analysis)"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-1-3-8","title":"1.3.8 — Why Sound Design is Not \"Writing the Melody\"","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.3.8 — Why Sound Design is Not \"Writing the Melody\"","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"A critical boundary in professional music production is separating <strong>sound design</strong> from <strong>melodic composition</strong>:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+------------------------------------+------------------------------------+\n|          MUSIC THEORY              |            SOUND DESIGN            |\n|       (Compositional Layer)        |         (Acoustic / Timbral Layer) |\n+------------------------------------+------------------------------------+\n| - Chord progressions &amp; harmony     | - Frequency spectrum distribution  |\n| - Melodic motifs &amp; phrasing        | - Harmonic density &amp; saturation    |\n| - Key signatures &amp; scales          | - Envelope dynamics (ADSR times)   |\n| - Rhythm &amp; meter (4/4, swing)      | - Modulations, filter movement, FX |\n+------------------------------------+------------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"A strong melody played on a poorly designed patch with conflicting low-end phase and harsh uncurbed resonances will ruin a club mix. Conversely, an extraordinary bass patch playing an uninspired, off-key sequence will fail to connect emotionally. "},{"type":"prose","html":"Mastery requires treating both disciplines with technical rigor while maintaining clear separation during production sessions: <strong>design the voice first, then compose the musical statement.</strong>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Ultimate Sound Design: Mastering Sound Design</em>, Section 1.11 (The Producer's Separation of Church and State)","<em>Sound-Design-Beginner-Knowledgebase.md</em>, §1"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]}]};
